<header><p class="enc-kicker">State</p><h1 class="enc-title">Duguwa dynasty</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">9th–11th century</span> · <a href="/centuries/9th-century">9th century</a> · Also known as Duguwa</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Duguwa were the first ruling dynasty of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-kanem">Kingdom of Kanem</a>, north-east of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lake-chad">Lake Chad</a>. Known from the royal chronicle of Kanem and from Arab geographers, who linked the kingdom with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zaghawa">Zaghawa</a> people, they ruled from around the ninth century until the late eleventh century. They were then displaced by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sayfawa-dynasty">Sayfawa</a>, who adopted <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/islam">Islam</a> as the religion of the court and ruled Kanem and later <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bornu">Bornu</a> for some eight centuries.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/duguwa-dynasty.jpg" alt="Duguwa dynasty" width="320" height="408" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Finden, Edward Francis, 1791-1857 -- Engraver · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Council_of_the_Sultan_of_Bornou_1826.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
